
Guidline for code development in AlTay
======================================

Version: $Rev$

Author: J. Gawad (Dept. CS, KU Leuven) jerzy.gawad@cs.kuleuven.be

Introduction
------------

The AlTay code has been growing organically for a couple of years. Some of its
components are probably older than 20 years now. Multiple authors have been
involved in the developments, including Paul Van Houtte himself, as well as
a host of his PhD students and postdocs.

At some point AlTay became overcomplicated and overly uncomprehesible. In the 
meantime the Fortran language has become much more friendly and expressive. 
Alas, the use of modern Fortran in AlTay was pretty limited.

An effort for improving that has been recently initiated. It includes:  

1. restructuring the existing code, with the goal to modernize its design,
   simplify/deobfuscate it and eliminate dead code,
2. adding new features.

Both parts of this endaveour require some guidance how to structure the new 
code. This brief document acts as a "good practice guide" for every new code
added to AlTay codebase.

The key words "MUST", "MUST NOT", "REQUIRED", "SHALL", "SHALL NOT", "SHOULD", 
"SHOULD NOT", "RECOMMENDED",  "MAY", and "OPTIONAL" in this document are to be
interpreted as described in [RFC 2119][rfc2119].

[rfc2119]: https://www.ietf.org/rfc/rfc2119.txt

Style guide for Fortran code
----------------------------

New developments of the AlTay should adhere to code standarts defined below.

### Layout of the source code ###

- Free-format source code must be used.

- Although Fortran standard allows a line to contain up to 132 characters (and
  some compilers permit even more), to improve readibility the code lines 
  should contain maximum 90 characters, preferably 80 characters. 

- Good indentation is crucial. Consistently use 4 spaces for indentation.
  Tabulators must not appear as indenters.

- Readability of the code is crucial. Leave at least 2 empty lines between the
  consequtive blocks of code, such as types, procedures, interfaces etc.

- All keywords should be written using lowercase letters.

- All names of Fortran intrinsics (functions and modules) should be written
  using lowercase letters.

- Double-colon `::` should be used in declarations of types, variables and
  constants, formal parameters, etc.

### Design principles ###

- The code must conform at least with Fortran 2003 standard.

- Implicit typing must not be used. Always use `implicit none`.

- The use of explicit literal constants (e.g. 6) is discouraged. If a constant
  appears in more than one context (this is typically the case),
  it should be turned into a named constant.
  Example: `integer,parameter :: TK_vector_dims = 6`

- In general, global state variables should be avoided. More specifically:
      * common blocks are *forbidden* and must not be used,
      * module variables with `save` attribute are discouraged, but they
		may be used if necessary,
      * local variables with `save` attribute (either explicit or implicit)
		are _strongly discouraged_ and should not be used unless a strong
		justification exists. If a variable with `save` is used, a relevant
		documentation must be provided.

### Naming conventions ###

- Naming convention for various elements of the code should follow the rules:
    * **files**: unless a good motivation is provided, any source file should
        be named after the module that is implemented within. Example: if the
        module is named `altayTK`, the file should be named `altayTK.f90`.
	* **modules**: use a prefix which is a lowercase name of the library
	  (or its abbreviation, or another name that is distinct enough) where the
	  module belongs to.
	* **types**: you may use one of two conventions: 
        1. if name of the type does not contain a prefix from the module it is
		   enclosed in, use camel-case. Example: `type(EulerAngles)`. Type names
		   without a prefix typically describe very generic concepts.
		2. if the name of the type is prefixed by the name of the module where it is
		   defined, you must use the name of the module literally. 
		   E.g. `type(altayStateContainer)` defined in the `altayState` module.
	* **constants (parameters)** defined in modules: it is advisable to use the same
	  prefix for all public parameters defined in a given module. The prefix 
	  should have some link to the module name. The prefix should be separated by
	  one underscore. Besides, underscores can be used if it improves readability.
	* **variables**: In general, lowercase names should be used. Underscores can be
	  used if it improves readability. 
	* **preprocessor macros**: use all-capitals, E.g. `PEBP_ENABLED`
	* **procedures**: 
         1. any module procedure (which applies, in fact, to almost all 
	        procedures in AlTay) should be named following the pattern
	        `<prefix>_name`, where the `<prefix>` is related either to the module 
	        name (or it is the module name itself), or the typename (see below).
         2. a module procedure that primarily operates on a given type should be given
            a name that follows the pattern: `<typename>_name`. Furthermore, its 
            first parameter should be of that type and named `this`.
      - Rationale #1: Fortran does not support namespaces. If one imports
           symbols from a module by means of the `use` construct, the symbols
           are imported into the global namespace. Of course, clashes between
           identical names defined in different modules are more than likely.
      - Rationale #2: this facilitates making type-bound procedures.
      - Rationame #3: this facilitates making generic procedures.
        E.g. `DeformationState_update`, where the typename is `DeformationState`
	* **interface procedures**: the names of dummy interface procedures follow the 
       pattern is `<prefix>_name_interface`. The prefix is determined from the rules 
       for naming procedures. If the dummy interface procedure is used for defining an
       deferred type-bound procedure inside a type (regardless the type is abstract or 
       not), the prefix must be set to that typename.
       E.g. `DeformationState_update_interface`, where the typename is 
       `DeformationState` and the procedure name is `update`.

### Procedures ###

- Generally, every subroutine must return exit status. The exit status should
  be included as the last non-optional formal parameter. 
  A conventional name for the exit status formal parameter is `info`. Named
  constansts from the `criErrcodes` module must be used for setting its values.

- Every formal parameter must be given an explicit intent (one of: `in`, `out`
  or `inout`).

- The following convention should be preferably followed in ordering the formal
  parameters:
	* optional parameters should be placed at the end.
	* parameters with intent "out" should appear as the last ones, but before the
	  optional parameter
	* if the procedure has an "out" parameter indended for returning its exit
	  code, it should appear as the last output parameter.
	* if the procedure basically operates on a given type, the object of that
	  type should be given as the very first parameter (even though it might
	  have the intent `inout`) and named `this`.
	  Example:

			subroutine altayTK_init(this, data_in, data_out, info, scaling)
			implicit none
			type(altayRun),intent(inout)				:: this
			double precision,dimension(TK_vector_dims)	:: data_in
			type(altayTKStateVariables),intent(out)		:: data_out
			integer,intent(out)							:: info
			double precision,intent(in),optional		:: scaling
			...
			end subroutine

- wherever possible, the procedures should be declared as pure.

### Documentation and comments ###

- Every publicly visible entity (module, type, procedure, named constant) must contain
  a short documentation. Doxygen-style comments must be used. The documentation
  line must precede the definition of the entity.
  Example:

		!> Length of field array in altayTKStateVariables
		integer,parameter :: TK_field_length

